<!--{"title": "Paying down technical debt without a rewrite: a field note", "description": "How a fractional CTO paydown plan works in practice: name the debt by its business cost, fund it like a portfolio and replace systems piece by piece instead of rewriting.", "h1": "Paying down technical debt without a rewrite: what works when the business cannot stop", "kicker": "Field note · Fractional CTO leadership", "lede": "Almost every company I join has a system everyone wants to rewrite. Almost none of those rewrites should happen. Debt is real and expensive, but the cure is usually a steady, funded paydown that the business can see, not a big-bang replacement that freezes product work for a year. This is how I approach it, and how I explain it to founders and boards.", "date": "2026-11-17", "datetime": "2026-11-17T21:20:00+04:00", "readtime": "8 min read", "format": "field-note", "tags": ["leadership", "architecture", "platform"], "keywords": ["technical debt", "paying down technical debt", "avoid big rewrite", "strangler fig pattern", "technical debt quadrant", "engineering investment allocation", "DORA metrics", "toil reduction", "fractional CTO technical debt", "legacy modernisation"], "faq": [["Should we rewrite our legacy system?", "Usually not all at once. Big rewrites tend to take longer than planned, freeze product work and recreate old problems. Replacing a system piece by piece, behind stable interfaces, keeps the business running and shows progress early."], ["How do I explain technical debt to a board?", "In business terms: what it slows down, what it puts at risk and what it costs in people's time. Tie each debt item to a measurable effect, such as release frequency, incident rate or hours of manual work."], ["How much engineering time should go to technical debt?", "There is no universal number. Agree an explicit share of capacity with the business, protect it, and adjust it based on evidence such as delivery speed and incident trends."]], "related": ["/